          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why is enteral nutrition preferred over parenteral nutrition in surgical patient</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why is enteral nutrition preferred over parenteral nutrition in surgical patients?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It delivers a higher caloric density per unit volume</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Caloric delivery depends on formulation, not route. Parenteral solutions can deliver more concentrated energy. The preference for enteral feeding rests on a different physiological advantage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It preserves the gut mucosal barrier and reduces bacterial translocation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Enteral feeding maintains mucosal cell integrity, preventing barrier failure and the translocation of bacteria into the bloodstream. This is why even a small trophic enteral feed is added alongside parenteral nutrition when possible.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It eliminates the risk of aspiration pneumonia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Enteral feeding via nasogastric tubes actually carries a risk of aspiration. Sitting the patient upright and using nasojejunal tubes reduce but do not eliminate this risk.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It avoids the need for daily electrolyte monitoring</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Electrolyte monitoring is required with both enteral and parenteral nutrition, particularly in patients at risk of refeeding syndrome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The cardinal rule is: if the gut works, use it. Enteral feeding preserves the mucosal barrier, reducing bacterial translocation and septic complications."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The gut mucosal barrier is the key reason — disuse atrophy allows bacterial translocation and systemic sepsis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
