          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which pair of assessments would most reliably have identified the gastric cancer</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which pair of assessments would most reliably have identified the gastric cancer patient's malnutrition risk before surgery?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum albumin and C-reactive protein</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Albumin is a negative acute-phase reactant that falls with inflammation regardless of nutritional intake. In chronic starvation (marasmus), albumin is often preserved, making it an unreliable screening tool.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Subjective Global Assessment and documented percentage weight loss</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The video identifies these as the two most valuable — and free — nutritional assessments. An SGA Category C grade indicates severe malnutrition, and unintentional weight loss exceeding 10% over 3–6 months is clinically significant. Together, they would have triggered preoperative nutritional optimisation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Body mass index and serum pre-albumin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>BMI can miss sarcopenic obesity and does not capture functional or protein status. Pre-albumin was not discussed as a key assessment tool in this module.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mid-arm circumference and handgrip dynamometry</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These are useful anthropometric and functional measures but are not the two assessments specifically highlighted in this module as the most valuable bedside tools.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The two most valuable nutritional assessments are free: documented percentage weight loss and a structured Subjective Global Assessment. Never rely on albumin to exclude malnutrition."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Weigh your patient and perform a Subjective Global Assessment — both are free, bedside-ready, and outperform any blood test."
                }
              ],
